## Side-Channel Attacks Explained

When it comes to side-channel attacks (**SCA**) there are multiple that pose serious threats in the circumstance of cryptographic messaging with two of the most popular being:

- **Timing Attacks**: small differences in execution time reveal information about inputs
- **Differential Power Analysis (DPA):** Analyzes detailed statistical measurements across multiple operations

In terms of threat rating **DPA** still is one of the most dangerous attacks from a black box perspective.

**Differential Power Analysis** is the process in which an attacker monitors variations in the electrical power consumption or electro-magnetic emissions of a target device. Once enough traces are collected, attackers apply statistical analysis to isolate patterns in power usage.  
Even the smallest correlations can reveal how bits are being processed, eventually exposing secret keys. Why this attack ends up so effective is because given enough traces even the smallest correlations can be isolated and with these patterns an attacker can extract information about gate switching, transistors turning on or off, or the interaction between one gate and another.

